# =============================================================================
# apps/api — PRODUCTION TEMPLATE. A checklist, not a configuration.
#
# NO PRODUCTION ENVIRONMENT EXISTS. RootLco runs locally and only locally
# (ADR-012). `.github/workflows/deploy-staging.yml` and `deploy-production.yml`
# are inert and reference no secrets. Nothing in this repository has ever been
# deployed, and this file is not evidence that anything has.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE IS: the exact set of values a deployment would have to be
# given, and WHO would have to give each one. Every entry is a marker:
#
#   <<FROM_OWNER: ...>>     a decision or an account only the Platform Owner
#                           can make or hold
#   <<FROM_PROVIDER: ...>>  a value a hosting or identity provider issues once
#                           the service is actually provisioned
#
# NO VALUE MAY BE INVENTED HERE. A stand-in that looks real is worse than a
# marker, because it gets copied. A marker cannot start a server, which is the
# point — `productionConfigurationProblems()` in
# src/server/config/backend-config.ts lists every required name that is still
# absent, and readiness reports `configuration.production-required` as failing
# until each one is supplied.
#
# NEVER commit the filled-in copy. NEVER point any value below at a local or a
# test service.
#
# Prose inventory, including what each value is for:
#   docs/platform/environment-configuration.md
#
# THE SINGLE LIST of everything only the Owner or a provider can supply is
# section 15 of that document. The markers below are the same set expressed
# per name, in the place you would fill them in; this file holds no second
# roll-call of them and neither should anything else.
# =============================================================================

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ENVIRONMENT IDENTITY
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# This is what switches the production-required check on, and it is the first
# segment of every storage key ever written. A key minted under the wrong token
# can never be corrected, only superseded.
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_ENV=production

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DATABASE — TWO DISTINCT LOGIN ROLES, NEVER ONE
#
# PostgreSQL resolves role membership at the login role, so a single role
# holding both app_runtime and app_platform would carry both authorities by
# inheritance on one connection and the containment would buy nothing. Two
# roles, two connection strings, no fallback between them.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Request path. Login role: member of app_runtime, NOSUPERUSER, NOBYPASSRLS.
# Readiness refuses a BYPASSRLS role outright.
DATABASE_URL=<<FROM_PROVIDER: connection string for the app_runtime login role>>

# Control plane. Login role: member of app_platform and of NO other application
# archetype. There is deliberately no fallback to DATABASE_URL.
PLATFORM_DATABASE_URL=<<FROM_PROVIDER: connection string for the app_platform login role>>

# Outbox worker. Falls back to DATABASE_URL when unset; give it its own
# app_worker login role instead, because the worker's policies are all-tenant.
# WORKER_DATABASE_URL=<<FROM_PROVIDER: connection string for the app_worker login role>>

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# IDENTITY PROVIDER
#
# All of these come from ONE hosted Supabase project and must agree with each
# other: a token minted by project A cannot be verified with project B's secret.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=<<FROM_PROVIDER: hosted Supabase project API URL>>
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=<<FROM_PROVIDER: hosted Supabase project anon key>>

# BYPASSES ROW-LEVEL SECURITY ENTIRELY. Treat as a root credential. Never give
# it the NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix, never log it, never put it in a client bundle.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=<<FROM_PROVIDER: hosted Supabase project service-role key>>

# Expected `iss` — the project API URL with /auth/v1 appended.
AUTH_JWT_ISSUER=<<FROM_PROVIDER: hosted Supabase project auth issuer>>

# Shared secret for HS* verification. The same project's JWT secret.
AUTH_JWT_SECRET=<<FROM_PROVIDER: hosted Supabase project JWT secret>>

# Expected `aud` and the algorithm allow-list. Both defaults are correct for a
# Supabase project; widen the algorithm list only deliberately.
AUTH_JWT_AUDIENCE=authenticated
AUTH_JWT_ALGORITHMS=HS256

# Exact, absolute destinations permitted for password-reset and invitation
# links, comma-separated. Empty rejects every one of them, so both flows fail
# closed until this names the public web origin's own URLs.
AUTH_REDIRECT_ALLOWLIST=<<FROM_OWNER: the public web origin's password-reset and invitation URLs>>

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HTTP EDGE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS is RESERVED and is therefore COMMENTED OUT. It is
# accepted and read by NOTHING: there is no CORS layer and no response-header
# layer on this tier, so no Access-Control-Allow-Origin header is emitted for
# any value written here. It is not required of a deployment and its absence
# cannot make this tier unready. Cross-origin access is a decision that needs a
# layer to enforce it first; until one exists there is nothing to configure.
# CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=

# Exact remote addresses of the reverse proxies in front of this tier,
# comma-separated. EMPTY means forwarded headers are ignored entirely, which is
# safe but makes every client look like the proxy for rate-limiting purposes.
# Supply the addresses only once the topology is known — a guess here hands
# every caller the ability to choose its own rate-limit identity.
TRUSTED_PROXY_IPS=<<FROM_PROVIDER: exact remote addresses of the reverse proxies, once a hosting target exists>>

# The limiter is the only protection the public routes have. `false` is listed
# as a configuration problem in production and is not a supported setting.
RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=true

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OBJECT STORAGE
#
# Attachments are not feature-gated: `unconfigured` refuses every call and
# `local_fake` signs against a .invalid host, so either one deployed is an
# outage of the whole attachment surface.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

STORAGE_PROVIDER=s3_compatible
STORAGE_S3_ENDPOINT=<<FROM_PROVIDER: S3-compatible endpoint URL>>
STORAGE_S3_REGION=<<FROM_PROVIDER: S3 region>>
STORAGE_S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=<<FROM_PROVIDER: S3 access key id>>
STORAGE_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=<<FROM_PROVIDER: S3 secret access key>>
STORAGE_S3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE=<<FROM_PROVIDER: true or false, per the endpoint's addressing style>>

# The bucket is provisioned out of band with its own policy, lifecycle and
# encryption. The adapter neither creates nor probes it outside local.
STORAGE_BUCKET=<<FROM_OWNER: the production bucket name>>

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# MESSAGE DELIVERY
#
# NO DELIVERY PROVIDER IS IMPLEMENTED. There is no adapter to select, so there
# is nothing to configure beyond the account the Owner would have to hold once
# one is built. Enqueueing works regardless: the outbound row is the durable
# record and delivery is the worker's separate concern.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# NOTIFICATION_PROVIDER=unconfigured
# <<FROM_OWNER: the delivery provider account and its credentials, once an
# adapter exists to use them>>

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RESERVED — ACCEPTED AND READ BY NOTHING
#
# Each name below is still accepted, so a deployment that already sets it keeps
# starting, but SETTING IT HAS NO EFFECT. None is required and none can make
# this tier unready. The authority is RESERVED_SETTINGS in
# apps/api/src/server/config/backend-config.ts, which a unit test checks against
# the real consumers, so this list cannot drift away from the code.
#
# CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS      — no CORS or response-header layer exists here.
# CACHE_DEFAULT_TTL_SECONDS — every cache caller passes its own TTL.
# DATABASE_REPLICA_URL      — no replica routing and no replica (ADR-012).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DELIBERATELY UNSET
#
# LOG_LEVEL — unset gives the `info` default; set it explicitly only if a
#             deployment wants something else.
#
# Every remaining name in apps/api/.env.example carries a bounded default that
# is correct for a deployment. Override one only with a reason.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
